Title:
NVDrv — Abusing NVIDIA nvoclock.sys for Physical Memory / Control Register Access

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
NVDrv is a Windows proof-of-concept that abuses the NVIDIA driver `nvoclock.sys` to gain arbitrary read/write primitives against physical memory. It also exposes functionality to manipulate CPU control registers (CR0–CR4 and others) and lays groundwork for MSR access (not yet implemented in the example). The project demonstrates how a vulnerable/abusable signed kernel driver can be used as a “bring your own vulnerable driver” (BYOVD) primitive to bypass kernel protections and enable powerful post-exploitation capabilities. It includes source code (C++), a sample loader script, and the driver binary to reproduce the behavior. This is primarily useful for red teamers, exploit developers, and security researchers studying kernel attack surface and driver abuse. It’s interesting because physical memory R/W and CR manipulation can be leveraged to disable security controls, tamper with kernel structures, or build stealthy kernel-level implants.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD via signed third-party kernel driver (`nvoclock.sys`)
- Arbitrary physical memory read/write primitives
- Control register (CR0–CR4) manipulation
- Kernel/user-mode IOCTL interaction patterns
- Foundations for MSR (Model-Specific Register) access

Use Cases:
- Kernel post-exploitation using a vulnerable signed driver
- Disabling or weakening kernel-enforced mitigations via CR modifications
- Reading/writing sensitive kernel memory structures for privilege escalation or stealth
- Security research on driver abuse detection and prevention
- Building PoCs for EDR/AV evasion and kernel tampering scenarios

Keywords:
NVDrv, nvoclock.sys, NVIDIA driver, BYOVD, signed driver abuse, Windows kernel, IOCTL, physical memory read, physical memory write, arbitrary R/W, control registers, CR0, CR3, CR4, MSR, kernel tampering, privilege escalation, driver exploitation, kernel security bypass, Windows internals